It's strange enough from a logical point of view that in a given game a
zinc wand has always the same functionality. Anyway, the (magical)
process of identifying it just reveals the mapping of zinc to this
functionality.

Whatever the perception of this association might be like, it is more
real to the character and she or he is _expected_ to remember it. This
is also because they have richer (visual, haptic, aural) perception and
are dealing with such important things as life and death, the avoidance
of the latter being sometimes courtesy of wands. The player however, may
forget these associations, and is thus assisted by the interface which
kindly replaces zinc with a more meaningful name, once the mapping has
been discovered.

How the mind flayers' psychic energy reaches this interface and/or
supposedly the player's mind is a bit of a mystery. Perhaps, as no real
forgetting is possible, this is a "best effort".

In any case, if I cannot be accused because I happen to remember what
ELBIB YLOH, HACKEM MUCHE, etc. stand for, why can I be accused if I
wrote it down? Esp., would you recommend players, in order to avoid
cheating, to hit `\' and memorize the entire list, when they see a mind
flayer coming? Also, would it be cheating if I turned off the "assisting
features" of the interface and let it use the descriptive names (ELBIB
YLOH, pink potion, ...) for everything (with the `\' command left
intact)? Then, I would, just as the real character, learn to distinguish
objects by their use and would have little problems remembering what my
stuff is (the game will be a bit[?] more tedious however).

